======================================================================================
 THE RULES (fine patterns):
======================================================================================

 [R1] 3-cores: 14 are FLAT (gap 2 = perfect (7,3,1) difference sets); 35-14=21 give gap 0.308.
      the flat 3-cores = QR {1,2,4} & QNR {3,5,6} and their 7 translates each:
      multiplier-orbit reps among flat 3-cores: [(0, 1, 3), (1, 2, 4), (1, 2, 6)]  (QR/QNR = Paley/Fano difference sets)

 [R2] DOUBLET {0,1}: spectrum [4.0, 3.247, 1.555, 0.1981, 0.1981, 1.555, 3.247] ; worst mode k*=[3, 4] (the MIDDLE freq, |1+zeta^3|^2=2+2cos6pi/7).
      ALL doublets {a,a+d} give the SAME gap 4cos^2(3pi/7) (any difference d!=0): the gap is
        difference d=1: gap=0.198062 worst k*=[3, 4]
        difference d=2: gap=0.198062 worst k*=[2, 5]
        difference d=3: gap=0.198062 worst k*=[1, 6]

 [R3] SINGLETON {0}: spectrum all 1 (flat!) gap=1 ; co-singleton (6-core) also gap 1. The 'unit' class.

 [R4] SPREAD: gap measures FLATNESS of the Fourier spectrum.
      FLATTEST (gap 2, all modes=2) = the difference sets (QR/Paley) = most 'spread'/random-like.
      MOST CONCENTRATED (gap 0.198, two-term) = the DOUBLET = the BINDING obstruction.
      => the apex obstruction is the LEAST-spread core (doublet), the OPPOSITE of the random/QR core.
